AB  - Dysphagia management requires texture-modified foods that ensure safe swallowing while providing adequate nutrition. However, traditional diets often lack sensory appeal and sufficient nutrition. Emulsion gels show promise for dysphagia patients as food ingredients to address these concerns. This review comprehensively demonstrates design strategies of emulsion gels to the specific needs of dysphagia patients, including swallowing safety, nutritional support, and sensory modulation. This review first outlines various emulsion gel formation techniques, including heat treatment, pH and salt induction, bridging flocculation, chemical treatment, enzymatic modification, and high internal phase emulsions. Subsequently, this review investigates their functions as advanced delivery systems for encapsulation, protection, and enhanced bioaccessibility of multiple hydrophobic bioactive compounds (such as beta-carotene, vitamins, and omega-3 fatty acids). Furthermore, the potential to modulate sensory perception during oral processing is also explored, including stimulating the swallowing reflex, masking unpleasant flavors, and enhancing saltiness and sweetness perception. This review also indicates future research directions such as the integration of three-dimensional printing and artificial intelligence for personalized nutrition and food manufacturing, as well as current challenges including lipid oxidation and the requirement for clinical validation and industrial production. This research provides theoretical insights for developing next generation foods for dysphagia patients by designing promising emulsion gels that coordinate texture, bioactives delivery, and sensory perception.
